<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Spirit of the Laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as The Spirit of Law, De l'esprit des lois, De l'esprit des loix</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spirit of the Laws (De l'esprit des lois) is a treatise on political theory and a pioneering work of comparative law by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, published anonymously in 1748 after about ten years of work. It argues that political institutions, to succeed, must reflect the social and geographical aspects of the particular community, and pleads for a constitutional system of government with separation of powers, preservation of law and civil liberties. Thomas Nugent published an English translation in 1750, and in 1751 the Church placed it on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index Librorum Prohibitorum</a>. It influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, the Founding Fathers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-spirit-of-the-laws.jpg" alt="The Spirit of the Laws" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Smuconlaw. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montesquieu,_De_l'Esprit_des_loix_(1st_ed,_1748,_vol_1,_title_page).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its constitution, praised by Montesquieu in <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b> (1748), influenced European reformers and the framers of the United States Constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1748 Montesquieu published <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b>, and in 1751 the first volume of the Encyclopédie appeared, edited by Denis Diderot and the mathematician Jean le Rond d’Alembert.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu’s <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b> (1748) distinguished monarchy, governed by fixed laws through intermediate powers such as the nobility and the parlements, from despotism, in which one man rules by his own will and caprice, and warned that a monarchy which destroyed those intermediate powers would become…</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu, in <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b> (1748), saw the nobility as the most natural of the “intermediate powers” that kept a monarchy from becoming a despotism, summing up its place in the maxim “no monarch, no nobility; no nobility, no monarch”.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu argued in <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b> (1748) that it is natural for a republic to have only a small territory, and Jean-Jacques Rousseau, who signed himself a citizen of Geneva, held in The Social Contract (1762) that a people could make its own laws only in…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu’s <b>The Spirit of the Laws</b> (1748) held up the English constitution, with its separation of legislative, executive and judicial power, as the model of political liberty.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
